## Wire the staged Generate workflow into API routes and merge (task 4 of staged ontology generate)

Context: Task 4 of the `staged-ontology-generate` plan on reviewed base
`be0e66e6` (Task 3 review fixes). Wires the Task 2 draft/contract layer
(`GenerateDraft.py`) and the Task 3 staged agent entry points (`staged.py`)
into an actual runnable workflow: detect -> human review -> checkpointed
completion -> append-only merge, exposed via new API routes. Also removes
every remaining production bridge that could still run the deprecated
one-shot `agent_owl_generator` path (and its post-generation pitfall-rewrite
loop), per the user's explicit instruction that pitfalls must never be
applied after generation. The attached implementation plan
(`/Users/benoit.cayla/.cursor/plans/staged-ontology-generate_6fc7e158.plan.md`)
was not edited. Followed strict TDD: `test_generate_workflow.py` and
`test_generate_routes.py` were written first and run to an observed RED
(`ModuleNotFoundError`/route-not-found) before `GenerateWorkflow.py` and the
new routes existed, then implemented to GREEN. Frontend templates/JS/CSS are
explicitly out of scope (Task 5); the existing wizard "Generate" button
still calls the now-410 legacy route and will need a Task 5 rewire.

Changes:

1. src/back/objects/ontology/GenerateWorkflow.py (new, 612 lines)
   Focused workflow service (kept out of the 2,500-line `Ontology.py` per
   the plan's explicit instruction), all pure orchestration — no FM call is
   made inline; every function that calls the staged agent is documented as
   expected to run inside a `TaskManager` worker thread.
   - `list_ready_document_manifests()` / `compute_current_fingerprint()` —
     reads only the durable parsed-corpus manifest (`DocumentParseService
     .status()`, never `parse_pending`/the extractor — no-reparse contract)
     plus the live ontology's locked-anchor identities, and recomputes the
     Task 2 `source_fingerprint` from them.
   - `run_detection()` — Stage 1: resolves locked anchors from
     `domain.get_classes()`, computes the fingerprint, calls
     `staged.detect_entities()`, then applies a defense-in-depth dedup pass
     against locked anchors by normalized canonical/alternate label **and**
     by the class name the merge would mint (`_sanitize_pascal`) — a second
     line of defense on top of `detect_entities()`'s own internal dedup, so
     this persistence boundary never depends on a caller/mock reproducing
     that behavior. Discards any prior draft and persists a fresh one,
     `stage=REVIEWING`, every new candidate `included=true` by default.
   - `get_draft_view()` / `update_draft()` / `discard_draft()` — Stage 2,
     fully synchronous (no LLM call). `update_draft()` handles `add` (manual
     candidate), `remove`, `update` (any of `canonical_label`,
     `description`, `type_hint`, `evidence`, `alternate_labels`, `included`),
     `include`, `exclude`, enforcing optimistic concurrency (`draft_revision`
     mismatch raises `DraftRevisionConflict`, 409) and delegating every
     invariant (locked-anchor immutability, unique labels, at-least-one-
     entity) to `GenerateDraft`'s existing Task 2 primitives.
   - `run_completion()` — Stage 3: rejects a stale draft
     (`ensure_not_stale`) or one not yet ready (`ensure_ready_for_completion`)
     before any LLM call; loops `next_pending_substage()` and runs
     `infer_relations` -> `infer_attributes` -> `infer_axioms` in that
     order, checkpointing `RUNNING` before each call and `DONE`/`FAILED`
     after, so a resumed run skips every substage already `done`. Adds a
     workflow-level entity-closure re-check (`_SUBSTAGE_REF_EXTRACTORS` +
     `draft.validate_references()`) immediately after each substage
     returns success and *before* checkpointing `DONE` — defense-in-depth
     against the substage's own internal check being bypassed by a test
     mock or a future staged.py bug; a failing re-check checkpoints `FAILED`
     and raises. On all three substages reaching `DONE`, calls
     `merge_draft_into_ontology()` then sets `stage=DONE`.
   - `merge_draft_into_ontology()` — the deterministic append-only merge.
     Existing classes/properties/`dataProperties` are read-only except for
     appends and one exception (setting a currently-unset `parent` from a
     `subClassOf` axiom); included candidates are minted a fresh,
     collision-free class `name` from `canonical_label` (`_sanitize_pascal`
     + `_unique_name`) and persisted via `alternate_labels` (new first-class
     field, see #2) — their detection-time `id` is the merge-time join key
     only (`id_to_name`), never carried into the live ontology as identity.
     Relations become new `ObjectProperty` entries (domain/range resolved
     through `id_to_name`); attributes become new `dataProperties` entries
     on the resolved class; axioms translate the staged shape
     (`kind`/`subject`/`object`) into the ontology's (`subClassOf` sets
     `parent`; `disjointWith`/`equivalentClass` append to `domain.axioms` as
     `{type, subject, objects}`). Any relation/attribute/axiom whose id
     doesn't resolve through `id_to_name` is dropped with a warning (already
     unreachable in practice — the entity-closure check above rejects it
     first) rather than silently corrupting the ontology. Calls
     `domain.clear_generated_content()` + `domain.record_change(...)` +
     `domain.save()`, matching the existing ontology-mutation contract.
2. src/back/objects/ontology/Ontology.py — `build_class_from_data()`
   Added `alternate_labels` as a first-class field on the ontology class
   dict (defaults to the existing value on edit, `[]` on create) — the
   persistence target `merge_draft_into_ontology()` writes synonyms into.
   Also removed `generate_with_agent()` (the legacy one-shot bridge to
   `agents.agent_owl_generator.engine.run_agent`, including its
   `TYPE_CHECKING`-only `AgentResult` import) — its only caller was the now-
   gated `/wizard/generate-async` route (#3), and the design requires the
   post-generation pitfall-rewrite loop it triggered to be unreachable from
   production Generate, not merely undocumented.
3. src/api/routers/internal/ontology.py
   - `POST /wizard/generate-async` (legacy) now always raises
     `OntoBricksError(..., status_code=410)` and does nothing else — no
     `require_domain_llm`, no `create_task`, no thread. Docstring points
     callers at the five new routes below.
   - `POST /wizard/generate/detect` — Stage 1, async (`TaskManager`
     background thread): resolves the LLM endpoint/warehouse via the
     existing `require_domain_llm`/`resolve_warehouse_id` helpers, then runs
     `GenerateWorkflow.run_detection()` in a worker thread exactly like the
     old generate-async route did; returns `{task_id}` for polling
     (`GET /tasks/{task_id}`) — the task result carries `{"draft": ...}`.
   - `GET /wizard/generate/draft` — Stage 2, synchronous: returns
     `GenerateWorkflow.get_draft_view()` (draft dict + a computed `stale`
     flag), or `{"draft": None}` if none is persisted.
   - `POST /wizard/generate/draft/update` — Stage 2, synchronous: validates
     `revision`/`op` are present, delegates to `GenerateWorkflow.
     update_draft()`, returns the updated draft (and its new revision).
   - `POST /wizard/generate/draft/discard` — Stage 2, synchronous: discards
     the persisted draft.
   - `POST /wizard/generate/complete` — Stage 3, async (`TaskManager`
     background thread): runs `GenerateWorkflow.run_completion()` (relations
     -> attributes -> axioms -> merge) in a worker thread; returns
     `{task_id}` — the task result carries `{"draft": ..., "merge": {
     classes_added, relations_added, attributes_added, axioms_added}}`.
   All five new routes reuse the existing `map_route_errors`/`OntoBricksError`
   mapping so `DraftValidationError`/`DraftRevisionConflict`/`DraftStaleError`
   /`NotFoundError` surface with their existing status codes (400/409/404)
   with no new error-handling wiring.
